  {
    "chunk_id": "d036bf6558cca995",
    "paper_id": "1751594c-9f12-43ff-8805-2741cfcce941",
    "text": "[3.5  Positional Encoding]\ns of input and output\npositions. Doing so requires a stack of O(n/k) convolutional layers in the case of contiguous kernels,\nor O(logk(n)) in the case of dilated convolutions [18], increasing the length of the longest paths\nbetween any two positions in the network. Convolutional layers are generally more expensive than\nrecurrent layers, by a factor of k. Separable convolutions [6], however, decrease the complexity\nconsiderably, to O(k · n · d + n · d2). Even with k = n, however, the complexity of a separabl",
    "source_section": "3.5  Positional Encoding",
    "title": "Attention Is All You Need",
    "authors": [
      "Ashish Vaswani",
      "Noam Shazeer",
      "Niki Parmar",
      "Jakob Uszkoreit",
      "Llion Jones",
      "Aidan N. Gomez",
      "Łukasz Kaiser",
      "Illia Polosukhin"
    ],
    "year": 2023,
    "chunk_index": 46
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "874507aea237656a",
    "paper_id": "1751594c-9f12-43ff-8805-2741cfcce941",
    "text": "[3.5  Positional Encoding]\nn · d2). Even with k = n, however, the complexity of a separable\nconvolution is equal to the combination of a self-attention layer and a point-wise feed-forward layer,\nthe approach we take in our model.\nAs side benefit, self-attention could yield more interpretable models. We inspect attention distributions\nfrom our models and present and discuss examples in the appendix. Not only do individual attention\nheads clearly learn to perform different tasks, many appear to exhibit behavior related to the syntacti",
    "source_section": "3.5  Positional Encoding",
    "title": "Attention Is All You Need",
    "authors": [
