  {
    "chunk_id": "c16c1bc64b4e6d31",
    "paper_id": "1992d589-827f-4654-b8ab-8877f68275c0",
    "text": "[3.5  Positional Encoding]\nion we\nconsider three desiderata.\nOne is the total computational complexity per layer. Another is the amount of computation that can\nbe parallelized, as measured by the minimum number of sequential operations required.\nThe third is the path length between long-range dependencies in the network. Learning long-range\ndependencies is a key challenge in many sequence transduction tasks. One key factor affecting the\nability to learn such dependencies is the length of the paths forward and backward signals have to",
    "source_section": "3.5  Positional Encoding",
    "title": "Attention Is All You Need",
    "authors": [
      "Ashish Vaswani",
      "Noam Shazeer",
      "Niki Parmar",
      "Jakob Uszkoreit",
      "Llion Jones",
      "Aidan N. Gomez",
      "Łukasz Kaiser",
      "Illia Polosukhin"
    ],
    "year": 2023,
    "chunk_index": 42
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "19f5fe264008de80",
    "paper_id": "1992d589-827f-4654-b8ab-8877f68275c0",
    "text": "[3.5  Positional Encoding]\nis the length of the paths forward and backward signals have to\ntraverse in the network. The shorter these paths between any combination of positions in the input\nand output sequences, the easier it is to learn long-range dependencies [12]. Hence we also compare\nthe maximum path length between any two input and output positions in networks composed of the\ndifferent layer types.\nAs noted in Table 1, a self-attention layer connects all positions with a constant number of sequentially\nexecuted operations, whe",
    "source_section": "3.5  Positional Encoding",
    "title": "Attention Is All You Need",
    "authors": [
      "Ashish Vaswani",
      "Noam Shazeer",
      "Niki Parmar",
      "Jakob Uszkoreit",
      "Llion Jones",
      "Aidan N. Gomez",
      "Łukasz Kaiser",
      "Illia Polosukhin"
    ],
    "year": 2023,
    "chunk_index": 43
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "a9de113391468f34",
    "paper_id": "1992d589-827f-4654-b8ab-8877f68275c0",
    "text": "[3.5  Positional Encoding]\nwith a constant number of sequentially\nexecuted operations, whereas a recurrent layer requires O(n) sequential operations. In terms of\ncomputational complexity, self-attention layers are faster than recurrent layers when the sequence\n\nlength n is smaller than the representation dimensionality d, which is most often the case with\nsentence representations used by state-of-the-art models in machine translations, such as word-piece\n[38] and byte-pair [31] representations. To improve computational performance f",
    "source_section": "3.5  Positional Encoding",
    "title": "Attention Is All You Need",
    "authors": [
      "Ashish Vaswani",
      "Noam Shazeer",
      "Niki Parmar",
      "Jakob Uszkoreit",
      "Llion Jones",
      "Aidan N. Gomez",
      "Łukasz Kaiser",
      "Illia Polosukhin"
    ],
    "year": 2023,
    "chunk_index": 44
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "5d6ccffc547a07e8",
    "paper_id": "1992d589-827f-4654-b8ab-8877f68275c0",
    "text": "[3.5  Positional Encoding]\nair [31] representations. To improve computational performance for tasks involving\nvery long sequences, self-attention could be restricted to considering only a neighborhood of size r in\nthe input sequence centered around the respective output position. This would increase the maximum\npath length to O(n/r). We plan to investigate this approach further in future work.\nA single convolutional layer with kernel width k < n does not connect all pairs of input and output\npositions. Doing so requires a stack of O(",
    "source_section": "3.5  Positional Encoding",
    "title": "Attention Is All You Need",
    "authors": [
      "Ashish Vaswani",
      "Noam Shazeer",
      "Niki Parmar",
      "Jakob Uszkoreit",
      "Llion Jones",
      "Aidan N. Gomez",
      "Łukasz Kaiser",
      "Illia Polosukhin"
    ],
    "year": 2023,
    "chunk_index": 45
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "08d4b88b342b9180",
    "paper_id": "1992d589-827f-4654-b8ab-8877f68275c0",
    "text": "[3.5  Positional Encoding]\ns of input and output\npositions. Doing so requires a stack of O(n/k) convolutional layers in the case of contiguous kernels,\nor O(logk(n)) in the case of dilated convolutions [18], increasing the length of the longest paths\nbetween any two positions in the network. Convolutional layers are generally more expensive than\nrecurrent layers, by a factor of k. Separable convolutions [6], however, decrease the complexity\nconsiderably, to O(k · n · d + n · d2). Even with k = n, however, the complexity of a separabl",
    "source_section": "3.5  Positional Encoding",
    "title": "Attention Is All You Need",
    "authors": [
      "Ashish Vaswani",
      "Noam Shazeer",
      "Niki Parmar",
      "Jakob Uszkoreit",
      "Llion Jones",
      "Aidan N. Gomez",
      "Łukasz Kaiser",
      "Illia Polosukhin"
    ],
    "year": 2023,
    "chunk_index": 46
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "b50b777aa27993f1",
    "paper_id": "1992d589-827f-4654-b8ab-8877f68275c0",
    "text": "[3.5  Positional Encoding]\nn · d2). Even with k = n, however, the complexity of a separable\nconvolution is equal to the combination of a self-attention layer and a point-wise feed-forward layer,\nthe approach we take in our model.\nAs side benefit, self-attention could yield more interpretable models. We inspect attention distributions\nfrom our models and present and discuss examples in the appendix. Not only do individual attention\nheads clearly learn to perform different tasks, many appear to exhibit behavior related to the syntacti",
    "source_section": "3.5  Positional Encoding",
    "title": "Attention Is All You Need",
    "authors": [
      "Ashish Vaswani",
      "Noam Shazeer",
      "Niki Parmar",
