  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "1a8cdce351154af8",
    "paper_id": "7b7dedb8-2bfd-41aa-98db-20b89c7e1efb",
    "text": "[4.3  Progressive coding]\nTable 1 also shows the codelengths of our CIFAR10 models. The gap between train and test is at\nmost 0.03 bits per dimension, which is comparable to the gaps reported with other likelihood-based\nmodels and indicates that our diffusion model is not overfitting (see Appendix D for nearest neighbor\nvisualizations). Still, while our lossless codelengths are better than the large estimates reported for\nenergy based models and score matching using annealed importance sampling [11], they are not\ncompetitive with ot",
    "source_section": "4.3  Progressive coding",
    "title": "Unknown",
    "authors": [],
    "year": null,
    "chunk_index": 52
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "e5bd9ace6d26a5c4",
    "paper_id": "7b7dedb8-2bfd-41aa-98db-20b89c7e1efb",
    "text": "[4.3  Progressive coding]\nealed importance sampling [11], they are not\ncompetitive with other types of likelihood-based generative models [7].\nSince our samples are nonetheless of high quality, we conclude that diffusion models have an inductive\nbias that makes them excellent lossy compressors. Treating the variational bound terms L1 +· · ·+LTas rate and L0 as distortion, our CIFAR10 model with the highest quality samples has a rate of 1.78\nbits/dim and a distortion of 1.97 bits/dim, which amounts to a root mean squared error of 0.9",
    "source_section": "4.3  Progressive coding",
    "title": "Unknown",
